ed state with 0/1 replicas available. The pods for "rate" are in CrashLoopBackOff, which means the pod is crashing repeatedly. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the "rate" service is failing. The next logical step is to check the logs of the failing pod. The "kubectl logs" command can help here. By getting the logs from the "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq" pod, they can see any error messages or stack traces that indicate what\'s causing the crashes. \n\nLooking at the available tools, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows running commands like logs. So, the command would be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation". This will retrieve the logs for the specific pod. If there are errors in the logs, like connection issues, configuration mistakes, or code exceptions, that would point to the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, if the logs don\'t show enough info, maybe checking the pod\'s events with "kubectl describe pod" could provide more context, like why the container is crashing. But since the user has a limited number of tool calls, starting with logs makes sense. If the logs are not helpful, they can proceed to describe the pod. \n\nAnother possibility is checking the deployment configuration for "rate" to see if there are any misconfigurations, but the initial step should be to gather runtime data first. So, the best tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the logs command.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The "rate" pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, indicating repeated crashes. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect its logs to identify errors, missing dependencies, or configuration issues. The command `kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation` will retrieve the container logs for the failing pod. This will reveal runtime errors, such as failed connections to databases, missing environment variables, or code-level exceptions.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 473, 'prompt_tokens': 3022, 'total_tokens': 3495, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb31-1e1d-7371-b0a1-8d1f5682d597-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3022, 'output_tokens': 473, 'total_tokens': 3495}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8e2e85e7-dbd7-4268-910f-b95497b37033'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the "rate" service in the hotel-reservation application is failing. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments and pods, and found that the "rate" deployment has a pod in CrashLoopBackOff. That means the pod is crashing repeatedly, which is why it\'s not available.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the logs of that specific pod. The user already used kubectl to get the pods list and identified the pod name as "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq". Now, they need to retrieve the logs from that pod to see what\'s causing the crashes. \n\nThe tool available for this is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which allows running kubectl commands. The command to get logs would be "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation". Since the pod name includes a random suffix (like -cpbgq), they need to use the exact name from the previous output. \n\nWait, in the previous response, the pod name was "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq". So the command should be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation". But the user has to make sure they use the correct pod name. Also, if the pod has multiple containers, they might need to specify the container name. However, the initial deployment for "rate" might have a single container. \n\nBy checking the logs, they can look for error messages that indicate why the container is crashing. Common issues could be application errors, failed dependencies (like a database connection), misconfigurations, or resource constraints. The logs should provide clues about the root cause. \n\nSo the tool call should be to execute the kubectl logs command with the specific pod name and namespace. This will retrieve the logs, which are essential for diagnosing the issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6e8315b5789e107', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 447, 'prompt_tokens': 4036, 'total_tokens': 4483, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb31-34be-7b23-99bd-8e5bd7caa5b8-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6e8315b5789e107', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4036, 'output_tokens': 447, 'total_tokens': 4483}), ToolMessage(content='{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"TLS disabled."}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"Set global log level: info"}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"Tune: setGCPercent to 100"}\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:24\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mReading config...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:37\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mRead database URL: mongodb-rate:27017\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:38\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mInitializing DB connection...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/db.go:39\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mNew session successfull...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/db.go:41\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mGenerating test data...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[31mFTL\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/db.go:45\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mnot authorized on rate-db to execute command { count: "inventory", query: { hotelId: "1" }, $db: "rate-db" }\x1b[0m\n', id='3e3b7493-3190-44fc-bd04-c29d0ebab62f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b6e8315b5789e107'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a2ac601d-01a6-4e2e-94f9-204674bd3b32'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where the "rate" service is crashing. From the logs, the error is about not being authorized on the MongoDB database. The log shows a command that\'s failing because the user doesn\'t have permission on the "rate-db" database.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the "rate" service can\'t authenticate with the MongoDB instance. The service is trying to connect to mongodb-rate:27017, which is probably a MongoDB instance in the same namespace. The error message mentions "rate-db" as the database name, so maybe the MongoDB instance is named rate-db, and the service is trying to connect to it.\n\nWait, the deployment for mongodb-rate is listed as running. So the MongoDB service is up. But the "rate" service can\'t authenticate. That suggests that the connection string or the credentials are incorrect. Maybe the username or password is wrong, or the user doesn\'t have the right permissions on the database.\n\nLooking at the logs again, the error is "not authorized on rate-db to execute command". So the user might not have the necessary permissions. The "rate" service might be using a service account that doesn\'t have access to the "rate-db" database. Alternatively, the MongoDB configuration might be set up with authentication, but the "rate" service isn\'t providing the correct credentials.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB service is not properly configured to allow connections from the "rate" service. For example, if the MongoDB instance is set up with a network policy that restricts access, the "rate" service might not be able to reach it. But since the "rate" pod is in the same namespace as the MongoDB pod, maybe the service is misconfigured.\n\nWait, the "rate" service is trying to connect to mongodb-rate:27017. The MongoDB service might be named mongodb-rate, but the actual database is called rate-db. So the connection string might be incorrect. The service name in Kubernetes is mongodb-rate, which would resolve to the service\'s endpoint. But if the MongoDB instance is configured to listen on a different port or the database name is different, that could cause issues.\n\nAlternatively, the MongoDB instance might require authentication, and the "rate" service isn\'t providing the correct credentials. The logs don\'t show any authentication errors, but the command is failing because the user isn\'t authorized. That suggests that the user exists but doesn\'t have the right permissions on the database.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the MongoDB configuration. Maybe the user in the "rate" service\'s configuration doesn\'t have access to the "rate-db" database. The user might need to be granted the necessary permissions, like read and write access to the "inventory" collection in the "rate-db" database.\n\nAnother angle: check the secrets or config maps used by the "rate" service. The credentials might be stored in a secret, and if that secret is misconfigured or missing, the service wouldn\'t have the correct username or password. The "rate" service\'s deployment might be referencing a secret that\'s not properly set up.\n\nAlternatively, the MongoDB instance might be using a different authentication mechanism, like SCRAM-SHA-256, and the "rate" service isn\'t configured to use that. But the logs don\'t mention authentication failure, just authorization. So the user exists but doesn\'t have the right permissions.\n\nTo confirm, I should check the MongoDB\'s access control settings. If the MongoDB instance is running with authentication enabled, the "rate" service needs the correct credentials. If the user is missing permissions, the solution would be to grant the necessary roles to the user in MongoDB.\n\nSo, the root cause is likely that the "rate" service\'s MongoDB user doesn\'t have the required permissions on the "rate-db" database. The fix would involve updating the MongoDB user\'s roles to include the necessary access.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The "rate" service is failing due to a MongoDB authorization error. To verify the connection details and credentials, I need to inspect the ConfigMap or Secret used by the "rate" deployment. This will reveal the database URL, username, and password. The command `kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation` and `kubectl get secret -n hotel-reservation` will help identify the configuration source. Since the error mentions "rate-db," I\'ll focus on secrets/configmaps related to MongoDB authentication.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 955, 'prompt_tokens': 3810, 'total_tokens': 4765, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_nam