ec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So I can run commands like \'kubectl get pods\' and \'kubectl logs\' for the specific pod.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment has a rollout status that\'s failed. Using \'kubectl describe deployment rate -n hotel-reservation\' could show more details about the deployment\'s status, events, and any errors. But the user\'s previous command was \'get deployments\', and the response included \'rate\' as 0/1. So the next step is to investigate why the \'rate\' deployment isn\'t running. Let me check the pods first. If there are no pods, maybe the deployment isn\'t creating them due to an image pull error, resource limits, or configuration issues. Also, checking the metrics for the \'rate\' service might show if it\'s not receiving traffic or if there are latency issues. But since the user\'s tools include get_metrics, I could use that with a query related to the \'rate\' service. However, the immediate step is to check the pods and their status. So the next tool call should be to get the pods in the namespace to see if the \'rate\' deployment has any pods, and if not, why. Then, check the logs if possible. But the user\'s available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so I can run \'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation\' to see the current state. That would help identify if the \'rate\' deployment has any pods, or if there\'s an issue with the deployment configuration. Alternatively, using \'kubectl describe deployment rate -n hotel-reservation\' would give more detailed info on the deployment\'s status. But since the user can only use the provided tools, I need to choose the most relevant one. The \'get pods\' command would be the first step to check the actual pod status. So the next tool call should be \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' with the command \'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation\' to see if the \'rate\' deployment has any pods and their statuses. If there are no pods, that\'s a sign of a problem. Then, further steps can be taken based on that information.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The "rate" deployment is in a failed state (0/1 replicas available). To diagnose this, I need to inspect the associated pods and their logs. First, I\'ll check the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace to identify if the "rate" service has any pods and their current status. This will help determine if the issue is with the deployment configuration, pod creation, or runtime errors. The command "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will provide this information.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 703, 'prompt_tokens': 2121, 'total_tokens': 2824, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb30-ed0c-71e2-8cc2-5a2e3e1cb211-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2121, 'output_tokens': 703, 'total_tokens': 2824}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4cb2d489-b674-4ed5-aadc-56d34c98bcb3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the "rate" deployment in the hotel-reservation namespace. From the previous step, they ran "kubectl get deployments" and noticed that the "rate" deployment has 0/1 replicas available, which means it\'s not running. The next logical step is to check the pods associated with that deployment to see why they aren\'t starting.\n\nSo, the user should use the "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" command. This will list all the pods in the namespace, including those related to the "rate" service. By checking the pod status, they can see if there are any errors, like crashes or failed pulls. If there are no pods listed for "rate", that might indicate a problem with the deployment configuration, such as incorrect image names or resource limits. If pods are present but in a CrashLoopBackOff state, there might be application errors or issues with the container\'s entry point. \n\nAdditionally, if the pod is pending, there could be issues with node resources or network problems. Once the pod status is known, the next step might involve checking the logs of the specific pod using "kubectl logs" to get more detailed error messages. But first, getting the list of pods is essential to understand the current state of the deployment.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93385900da9c025a', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 304, 'prompt_tokens': 3137, 'total_tokens': 3441, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb31-0d27-70d0-a405-dd2d90e5b57c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93385900da9c025a', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3137, 'output_tokens': 304, 'total_tokens': 3441}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS             RESTARTS       AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-hhpms                   1/1     Running            0              2m39s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-qg4gx                 1/1     Running            0              2m39s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-f2lhx                      1/1     Running            2 (115s ago)   2m38s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-kgw2s                   1/1     Running            0              2m38s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-ghxx8         1/1     Running            0              2m37s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-mw9ck            1/1     Running            0              49s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-lhf8r        1/1     Running            0              2m31s\nmongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-wll77               1/1     Running            0              2m38s\nmongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-4z4wh          1/1     Running            0              2m36s\nmongodb-rate-bfbcf4587-4zc7h              1/1     Running            0              2m35s\nmongodb-recommendation-674dddbcdf-jnlt5   1/1     Running            0              2m34s\nmongodb-reservation-6cdf69dcc-7hbwh       1/1     Running            0              2m30s\nmongodb-user-6dcf4877-cfgrt               1/1     Running            0              2m23s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-4gmd8                  1/1     Running            1              2m36s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq                     0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   2 (21s ago)    46s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-snr6q           1/1     Running            2 (98s ago)    2m33s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-k64ql              1/1     Running            2 (108s ago)   2m28s\nsearch-5f668579c9-xrbl6                   1/1     Running            0              2m26s\nuser-6548d868cd-wfkd5                     1/1     Running            2 (91s ago)    2m21s\nwrk2-job-8s7hl                            1/1     Running            0              50s\n', id='4939d428-75df-415e-b1e7-35be45e26a22', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-93385900da9c025a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a56da213-b6d8-4f12-a08b-aefa3499d39f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. From the previous steps, they found that the "rate" deployment is in a failed state with 0/1 replicas available. The pods for "rate" are in CrashLoopBackOff, which means the pod is crashing repeatedly. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the "rate" service is failing. The next logical step is to check the logs of the failing pod. The "kubectl logs" command can help here. By getting the logs from the "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq" pod, they can see any error messages or stack traces that indicate what\'s causing the crashes. \n\nLooking at the available tools, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows running commands like logs. So, the command would be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation". This will retrieve the logs for the specific pod. If there are errors in the logs, like connection issues, configuration mistakes, or code exceptions, that would point to the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, if the logs don\'t show enough info, maybe checking the pod\'s events with "kubectl describe pod" could provide more context, like why the container is crashing. But since the user has a limited number of tool calls, starting with logs makes sense. If the logs are not helpful, they can proceed to describe the pod. \n\nAnother possibility is checking the deployment configuration for "rate" to see if there are any misconfigurations, but the initial step should be to gather runtime data first. So, the best tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the logs command.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The "rate" pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, indicating repeated crashes. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect its logs to identify errors, missing dependencies, or configuration issues. The command `kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation` will retrieve the container logs for the failing pod. This will reveal runtime errors, such as failed connections to databases, missing environment variables, or code-level exceptions.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 473, 'prompt_tokens': 3022, 'total_tokens': 3495, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb31-1e1d-7371-b0a1-8d1f5682d597-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3022, 'output_tokens': 473, 'total_tokens': 3495}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8e2e85e7-dbd7-4268-910f-b95497b37033'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the "rate" service in the hotel-reservation application is failing. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments and pods, and found that the "rate" deployment has a pod in CrashLoopBackOff. That means the pod is crashing repeatedly, which is why it\'s not available.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the logs of that specific pod. The user already used kubectl to get the pods list and identified the pod name as "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq". Now, they need to retrieve the logs from that pod to see what\'s causing the crashes. \n\nThe tool available for this is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which allows running kubectl commands. The command to get logs would be "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation". Since the pod name includes a random suffix (like -cpbgq), they need to use the exact name from the previous output. \n\nWait, in the previous response, the pod name was "rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq". So the command should be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation". But the user has to make sure they use the correct pod name. Also, if the pod has multiple containers, they might need to specify the container name. However, the initial deployment for "rate" might have a single container. \n\nBy checking the logs, they can look for error messages that indicate why the container is crashing. Common issues could be application errors, failed dependencies (like a database connection), misconfigurations, or resource constraints. The logs should provide clues about the root cause. \n\nSo the tool call should be to execute the kubectl logs command with the specific pod name and namespace. This will retrieve the logs, which are essential for diagnosing the issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6e8315b5789e107', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 447, 'prompt_tokens': 4036, 'total_tokens': 4483, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb31-34be-7b23-99bd-8e5bd7caa5b8-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-cpbgq -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6e8315b5789e107', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4036, 'output_tokens': 447, 'total_tokens': 4483}), ToolMessage(content='{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"TLS disabled."}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"Set global log level: info"}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T09:46:45Z","message":"Tune: setGCPercent to 100"}\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:24\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mReading config...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:37\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mRead database URL: mongodb-rate:27017\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T09:46:45Z\x1b[0m \x1